{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "tr-02-platforms-bridges-tunnels",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Platforms, Bridges, and Tunnels",
    "description": "Extend the crossing model to long stationary objects by adding the train length to the object length.",
    "teachingApproach": "Begin with the visual sum-of-lengths model, then reverse it to find platform or train length, extend to tunnels and length multiples, and finish with the extra-time and two-platform subtraction shortcuts.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 20, Problems on Trains. Approved scope: Teach that to completely cross a platform, bridge, or tunnel, the train's front must travel the train length plus the object's length. Use this to find crossing time, train length, platform/bridge/tunnel length, and speed. Cover objects that are a multiple of the train length, and develop the extra-time shortcut: compared with crossing a pole, the additional time is exactly the time needed to cover the platform or bridge length. Include two-platform comparisons where the difference in crossing times corresponds to the difference in platform lengths."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A pole is only a point. A platform has length.\n\nThat one change creates most train-length questions: the front must travel far enough for the entire train to clear both its own length and the object's length.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A platform changes one thing: the train must clear an object that also has length.\n\nPicture a 160 m train approaching a 240 m platform. From the instant the front reaches the platform until the rear leaves it, the front travels 160 + 240 = 400 m.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "So the rule is:\n\nextended stationary object → distance = train length + object length\n\nAt 72 km/hr = 20 m/s, 400 m takes 20 s.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
